1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,944
How does our system talk to Uzcard and Humo, when they speak different languages?

2
00:00:04,994 --> 00:00:13,704
Uzcard's SV-Gate speaks JSON-RPC, and Humo speaks SOAP for payments and REST for cards, each with its own fields and codes.

3
00:00:13,754 --> 00:00:21,000
If the payment code knew both, every new scheme would mean touching payment code, and no other project could reuse the work.

4
00:00:21,050 --> 00:00:29,496
So each scheme gets its own adapter: a separate Spring Boot service with its own OpenAPI contract, that any service or project may call.

5
00:00:29,546 --> 00:00:38,904
Every adapter answers plain REST with one envelope: a request id, the scheme's own status, and its data in the scheme's own field names.

6
00:00:38,954 --> 00:00:48,768
Inside, every scheme method is one command, picked by its type from a factory, calling the scheme through typed models and one HTTP interface client per area.

7
00:00:48,818 --> 00:00:57,144
What each scheme code means lives in a status table: success, error, pending, not found, unavailable, or manual review.

8
00:00:57,194 --> 00:01:04,992
The card module turns a scheme's decline code into its own: insufficient funds, expired card, or limit exceeded.

9
00:01:05,042 --> 00:01:11,280
Uzcard debits in one step, by the card's token, with our payment id as the external reference.

10
00:01:11,330 --> 00:01:19,656
If that reference was sent before, SV-Gate refuses it as a duplicate, the adapter answers pending, and a lookup by the reference tells the outcome.

11
00:01:19,706 --> 00:01:27,864
Humo holds the money first, and the card module captures it right away; a hold whose capture got no answer is captured when its status is asked.

12
00:01:27,914 --> 00:01:36,984
Humo settles a terminal's payments only after reconciliation, so the card module reconciles every Humo e POS terminal before each clearing session.

13
00:01:37,034 --> 00:01:47,088
The app wires each adapter's generated clients as one HTTP service group, and the card module sees only one port, a card processor, per scheme.

14
00:01:47,138 --> 00:01:53,352
Every answer becomes one of three outcomes: approved, declined with our own code, or unknown.

15
00:01:53,402 --> 00:02:05,376
A timeout, a redirect, or an answer it cannot read is the adapter's client error, a five oh two, and the card module reads it as unknown, never a decline, because the money may already have moved.

16
00:02:05,426 --> 00:02:09,288
Next, knowing a card's scheme, and storing its number safely.
